# ReachAI — AI Agent Rules
# Use this file with Cursor, Copilot, Claude Code, or any AI coding agent.
# These rules keep the agent focused, consistent, and aligned with the build plan.

---

## Project identity
This is a Chrome extension (Manifest V3) that acts as an agentic AI assistant
for LinkedIn DMs and Gmail. It uses React + Vite for the extension UI,
Node.js + Hono for the backend, OpenAI GPT-4o for AI, Supabase for the database,
and Paddle for payments. It is deployed on Digital Ocean.

---

## Golden rules — never break these

1. NEVER call the OpenAI API directly from the extension.
   All AI calls go through the backend. The API key must never touch the browser.

2. NEVER hardcode secrets, API keys, or tokens anywhere in the codebase.
   All secrets live in backend/.env only.

3. NEVER commit .env files. Only commit .env.example with placeholder values.

4. NEVER use Manifest V2 APIs. This project uses Manifest V3 only.
   Use service workers, not background pages.
   Use chrome.scripting, not chrome.tabs.executeScript (deprecated).

5. NEVER add a new dependency without a clear reason.
   Ask before installing packages not already in package.json.

6. NEVER skip error handling. Every async function must have try/catch.
   Every API response must check for error status before using data.

7. NEVER mix popup logic with content script logic.
   Popup = UI only. Content scripts = DOM reading/injection only.
   Communication between them goes through chrome.runtime.sendMessage only.

8. NEVER store sensitive user data (email content, message text) in
   chrome.storage.local beyond what is needed for the current session.
   Draft queue items should expire after 7 days maximum.

---

## Build approach — vertical, one feature at a time

Always complete one feature fully before starting the next.
A feature is DONE only when:
  - Backend route works and is tested
  - Extension UI renders correctly
  - Error states are handled
  - It works end-to-end in the actual browser

Current build order:
  [1] Extension shell + Google auth        ← START HERE
  [2] Gmail thread reader + AI draft
  [3] Draft queue dashboard
  [4] LinkedIn DM reader + AI draft
  [5] Auto-categorize conversations
  [6] Follow-up reminders

Do not start feature [N+1] until feature [N] is fully working.

---

## Code style

Language: JavaScript (no TypeScript for now — keep it lean)
Formatting: 2-space indentation, single quotes, semicolons
Components: functional React only, hooks only — no class components
Naming:
  - React components: PascalCase (DraftCard.jsx)
  - Functions and variables: camelCase
  - Constants: UPPER_SNAKE_CASE
  - Files: camelCase for non-components, PascalCase for React components
  - API routes: kebab-case (/draft-queue not /draftQueue)

---

## File responsibilities — do not mix these

extension/src/popup/         → UI components only. No direct DOM access.
extension/src/content/       → DOM reading and injection only. No UI state.
extension/src/background/    → Alarms, notifications, message routing. No UI.
extension/src/lib/api.js     → ALL backend fetch calls. Nowhere else.
extension/src/lib/auth.js    → ALL chrome.identity OAuth logic. Nowhere else.
backend/src/services/openai.js → ALL GPT-4o calls. Never in routes directly.
backend/src/lib/prompts.js   → ALL system prompts. Never inline in service files.

---

## Backend route conventions

Every route must:
  1. Run auth middleware first (verify user token)
  2. Validate request body before processing
  3. Return consistent JSON shape: { success: true, data: {} } or { success: false, error: "message" }
  4. Use HTTP status codes correctly (200, 201, 400, 401, 403, 500)

Example:
  POST /drafts/generate
  Body: { threadId, platform, messages[] }
  Response: { success: true, data: { draftId, text, platform, contact } }

---

## GPT-4o usage rules

Model: gpt-4o (never gpt-3.5, never gpt-4-turbo unless specified)
All prompts live in: backend/src/lib/prompts.js
Always include a system prompt. Never send user content as system prompt.
Max tokens: 500 for drafts, 150 for categorization
Temperature: 0.7 for drafts, 0.2 for categorization (deterministic labels)
Always handle OpenAI rate limits with exponential backoff (max 3 retries)

---

## Chrome extension specific rules

Permissions: only request what is actually used. No over-permissioning.
Content Security Policy: no inline scripts, no eval()
LinkedIn content script: use MutationObserver for SPA navigation detection.
  LinkedIn does not do full page reloads — your script must watch for URL changes.
Gmail content script: wait for Gmail's dynamic compose box to mount before injecting.
  Use a MutationObserver on document.body with a debounce of 300ms.
chrome.storage: use chrome.storage.local for user session data.
  Use chrome.storage.sync only for small user preferences (< 8KB limit).

---

## Supabase rules

Use the Supabase service key ONLY on the backend. Never in the extension.
The extension authenticates users via Google OAuth, then sends the token to backend.
Backend verifies the token and issues its own session.
Tables to keep in mind:
  users         (id, email, google_id, plan, created_at)
  drafts        (id, user_id, platform, thread_id, text, status, created_at)
  thread_labels (id, user_id, thread_id, platform, label, updated_at)
  reminders     (id, user_id, thread_id, platform, due_at, status)

---

## What to do when stuck

1. Check the PROJECT_STRUCTURE.md for where something belongs
2. Check backend/src/lib/prompts.js before writing a new prompt
3. Check extension/src/lib/api.js before writing a new fetch call
4. Re-read the golden rules before adding a new pattern
5. Ask: "Does this belong in popup, content, or background?" before writing code

---

## Out of scope for MVP (do not build yet)

- Autonomous sending without user approval
- Twitter/X integration
- Calendar API integration
- Team/multi-user accounts
- Mobile app
- Any Tier 2 or Tier 3 agentic features
- Vector database or LangChain
- Analytics (PostHog, Sentry) — add after MVP ships
